    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • Project KO11 Is Proof That Geely Wants To \"Knock Out\" Range Rover, Launches Next Year in Europe\nElon Musk Confirms That Special Committees From Tesla and SpaceX Are Already Working on Merger Plans\n8 photos\nPhoto: SpaceX\nDuring the SpaceX third-quarter earnings call, Elon Musk confirmed that special committees from Tesla and SpaceX are already at work to draw up a merger plan. He denied being involved or up to date with the talks, as he considered himself a \"conflicted party.\" Analysts believe a Tesla-SpaceX merger will be announced by the end of the year, possibly during Tesla's shareholder meeting on December 3.\nEarlier this week, Tesla announced that its annual shareholder meeting is scheduled for December 3. Although rumors about a merger with SpaceX have been floating around for some time, shareholders were not briefed on such plans. Instead, the proxy statement invites them to vote on re-electing three directors (Musk included), the 2025 executive compensation package (Musk's included), and naming PwC as Tesla's auditor for 2026.\nNobody expected Tesla to put such a delicate matter in front of its shareholders without preparing them first. Shareholder approval for a SpaceX merger is crucial, and Tesla's supermajority voting rule makes it very difficult to accomplish. This is the reason why Tesla introduced an auto-vote measure, which should take effect starting with the next shareholder meeting (not the one on December 3).\nHowever, the merger is very much on the agenda at the meeting, especially as Elon Musk officially confirmed the plans on Tuesday at the SpaceX third-quarter earnings call. Musk revealed that both Tesla and SpaceX have formed special committees to explore a merger. He denied that he has any knowledge of what these committees are discussing because he's \"a conflicted party.\"\n\"The special committees have not reached an agreement on any transaction, and it doesn't mean they will, but there is a lot to be gained from cooperating on SI, chip design, robots, mass or orbit, connectivity,\" Musk told investors during the earnings call. He also said that he thinks a merger is a good idea, but the shareholders will have to support it.\nKnowing Musk and his influence with the Board of Directors, I would say he has more than an idea of where these discussions are heading. I can also venture to say that he considers it a done deal, or he wouldn't have talked about it publicly. In an earnings call, no less, especially as he repeatedly declined to talk about a merger during such calls in the past. Musk admitted as much, saying that even commenting on a possible merger felt like \"tap dancing through a minefield.\"\nAnalysts and investors think a merger might happen sooner than Musk let us believe. Dan Ives, former Global Head of Technology Research at Wedbush Securities, believes there's an 80%-90% chance that Tesla and SpaceX merge by the end of 2027. That doesn't mean that it will happen overnight. Tesla will have to first sort out regulatory hurdles and get shareholders from both companies on board. That's an easy task for SpaceX, but Musk doesn't control Tesla.\nThat's why I believe Musk will have to inform Tesla shareholders of the merger plans as soon as possible. The best opportunity will be, of course, the December 3 shareholder meeting . The auto-voting procedure introduced now will allow shareholder to automatically vote their shares with the board's recommendations. That should avoid situations where not enough shares are available to pass a vote, let alone at the Board's recommendation.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\ntesla-spacex merger Elon Musk spacex earnings call Tesla\nAbout the author: Cristian Agatie\nAfter his childhood dream of becoming a \"tractor operator\" didn't pan out, Cristian turned to journalism, first in print and later moving to online media. His top interests are electric vehicles and new energy solutions. Full profile\nUP NEXT This Shelby GT350 Just Sold for Hilariously Cheap – But There’s a Catch",
